  "generated_text": "1. **Calculate the initial number of license plates:**\n   - The first letter has 5 choices.\n   - The second letter has 3 choices.\n   - The third letter has 4 choices.\n   - Total initial license plates = $5 \\times 3 \\times 4 = 60$.\n\n2. **Determine the effect of adding two letters:**\n   - If both letters are added to the first set, the first letter has 7 choices, and the total number of license plates becomes $7 \\times 3 \\times 4 = 84$.\n   - If one letter is added to the first set and one to the second set, the first letter has 6 choices, the second letter has 4 choices, and the third letter has 4 choices. The total number of license plates becomes $6 \\times 4 \\times 4 = 96$.\n\n3. **Calculate the additional license plates:**\n   - Additional license plates = $96 - 60 = 36$.\n   - Additional license plates = $84 - 60 = 24$.\n\nThe largest possible number of ADDITIONAL license plates obtainable is $\\boxed{36}$.",
  "grade": {
    "error": null,
    "extracted_answer": "[36, '36']",
    "gold_answer": "Before new letters were added, five different letters could have been chosen for the first position, three for the second, and four for the third. This means that $5\\cdot 3\\cdot 4=60$ plates could have been made.\n\nIf two letters are added to the second set, then $5\\cdot 5\\cdot 4=100$ plates can be made. If one letter is added to each of the second and third sets, then $5\\cdot 4\\cdot 5=100$ plates can be made. None of the other four ways to place the two letters will create as many plates. So, $100-60=\\boxed{40}$ ADDITIONAL plates can be made.\n\nNote: Optimum results can usually be obtained in such problems by making the factors as nearly equal as possible.",
